"Look around your classroom. Every person in it, and every person on Earth, belongs to one species, Homo sapiens. The oldest fossils of our species found so far come from Africa, and they are about 300,000 years old. For most of the time since then, almost all of our ancestors lived in Africa. Today people live on every continent except Antarctica, on high mountains and on small islands far out in the ocean. How did we get everywhere? Nobody set out to travel the world. A group might move a short way to follow the animals it hunted, to find fresh water, or to make room for a growing family. Their grandchildren moved a little farther. Over thousands of years, these small steps added up to whole continents. Some groups left Africa very early, perhaps more than 100,000 years ago. But researchers think that most people outside Africa today descend mainly from groups that left later, roughly 70,000 to 50,000 years ago. The world those people walked into was colder than ours. During the last ice age, so much water was frozen into huge ice sheets that the sea stood far lower than it does today: more than 120 meters lower at its lowest point, about 21,000 years ago. Shallow seas became dry land. Asia and North America were joined by a wide plain called Beringia. Australia and New Guinea were one landmass. Taiwan was part of the Asian mainland. Land bridges did not reach everywhere. Between Southeast Asia and Australia the water stayed deep, so the first people to reach Australia must have crossed open sea, at least 50,000 years ago and perhaps 65,000 years ago. People were living in Europe by about 45,000 years ago. Most researchers think they had reached the Americas by about 15,000 years ago, and footprints found in North America may be 23,000 years old. The last large lands to be settled were islands deep in the Pacific. Voyagers reached New Zealand only about 750 years ago. How can anyone know what happened before writing existed? Researchers use three kinds of evidence. Fossils show what people's bodies were like. Stone tools show where people lived, even in places where no bones have survived. DNA, taken from living people and sometimes from very old bones, shows how groups are related. None of the three gives an exact date. That is why this lesson keeps saying “about,” and why it gives a range instead of a single number. Our species was not alone. Other kinds of humans, such as the Neanderthals, were already living in Europe and Asia. They were not our ancestors in a straight line, but close relatives, and DNA shows that the two groups sometimes had children together. The story is still being rewritten. Until 2017, the oldest known fossils of our species were about 195,000 years old; then a find in Morocco pushed that back by 100,000 years. The next discovery may change this lesson too.": "0349d2cec892",
